diagnosis_text:
After exhaustive investigation of every device and interface in the reported path, the network is functioning correctly. PC4 (172.16.10.4/24) on VLAN 10 and Laptop0 (172.16.20.10/24) on VLAN 20 are separated at Layer 2 by switch1, which correctly places PC4's port (eth1) into the br_vlan10 bridge and Laptop0's port (eth7) into the br_vlan20 bridge. The trunk link between switch1 and mswitch1 carries 802.1Q-tagged frames for both VLANs correctly via sub-interfaces (eth0.10 → br_vlan10, eth0.20 → br_vlan20). mswitch1 acts as a pure Layer 2 forwarding device, shuttling tagged frames to router0 via its eth4 trunk (eth4.10 for VLAN 10, eth4.20 for VLAN 20). Router0 serves as the inter-VLAN router with eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24) configured, and its routing table has both subnets as directly connected. End-to-end pings succeed in both directions with TTL=63 (one hop, as expected), return-path ARP is valid, router0 shows zero drops and zero errors on both VLAN sub-interfaces, and all bridge port states are "forwarding." No fault is present in the current running state. The user's symptom may have been caused by a transient condition such as a device restart with a brief MAC learning delay or an ARP cache staleness event that has since self-resolved.
=== END ===